<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-laplace-pfd-repeated" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Inverse Laplace with a Repeated Linear Factor</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       TABLE REFERENCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Keep the Laplace transform table handy as a reference for this page.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-laplace-ref-4-2-4">
    <title>Laplace Transform Table</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>F(s) = \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}</m></cell>
        <cell>Condition</cell>
        <cell>Formula</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>A</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s-a}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>B</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>C</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\sin(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{a}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>D</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\cos(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>E</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>H</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\sin(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{b}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>I</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\cos(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>J</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CONCEPTUAL MC BEFORE ACTIVITY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Before we start, think about why the repeated factor <m>s^2</m> leads to two separate
    fractions in the decomposition rather than one.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-repeated-conceptual">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why does the repeated linear factor <m>s^2</m> contribute two separate fractions
        in the partial fraction decomposition of <m>\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because <m>s^2 = s \cdot s</m>, meaning <m>s</m> appears twice as a linear factor.
            Each power of <m>s</m> needs its own fraction with a constant numerator.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! A repeated linear factor <m>(s-r)^n</m> contributes one fraction for each
            power from 1 up to <m>n</m>. Here <m>s^2</m> gives a fraction for <m>s</m>
            and a separate one for <m>s^2</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because <m>s^2</m> is an irreducible quadratic, so it needs a linear numerator <m>Bs + C</m>,
            which accounts for two unknowns.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            <m>s^2</m> is not irreducible; it factors as <m>s \cdot s</m>.
            Irreducible quadratics are ones with no real roots, like <m>s^2 + 4</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because the degree of the numerator must equal the degree of the denominator.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The number of terms in a partial fraction decomposition is determined by the factors
            of the denominator, not by matching degrees.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because <m>s^2</m> has two complex roots, each requiring its own fraction.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            <m>s^2 = 0</m> has the real root <m>s = 0</m> repeated twice, not two complex roots.
            It is the repetition of this real root that gives two fractions.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-inverse-laplace-repeated">
    <title>Find <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1}{s^3+4s^2}\right\}</m></title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Notice that <m>s^3 + 4s^2 = s^2(s+4)</m>, where <m>s</m> is a repeated linear factor.
        This expression does not appear in the table, so we use partial fraction decomposition.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ── TASK 1: PFD ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-repeated-pfd">
      <title>Step 1: Partial Fraction Decomposition</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          From the review page, the correct form for a repeated linear factor is:
          <me>\frac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \frac{A}{s} + \frac{B}{s^2} + \frac{C}{s+4}.</me>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Solving for A, B, and C (can skim, reference for homework)</title>
          <p>
            Multiply both sides by <m>s^2(s+4)</m> to clear denominators:
            <me>1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.</me>
            Expand:
            <md>
              <mrow>1 \amp= As^2 + 4As + Bs + 4B + Cs^2</mrow>
              <mrow>\underbrace{0}_{\text{coeff of }s^2} \cdot s^2
                + \underbrace{0}_{\text{coeff of }s} \cdot s
                + \underbrace{1}_{\text{const}}
                \amp= \underbrace{(A+C)}_{\text{coeff of }s^2} s^2
                   + \underbrace{(4A+B)}_{\text{coeff of }s} s
                   + \underbrace{4B}_{\text{const}}.</mrow>
            </md>
            Matching coefficients:
            <md>
              <mrow>A + C \amp= 0</mrow>
              <mrow>4A + B \amp= 0</mrow>
              <mrow>4B \amp= 1.</mrow>
            </md>
            From the third equation <m>B = \dfrac{1}{4}</m>.
            From the second, <m>A = -\dfrac{B}{4} = -\dfrac{1}{16}</m>.
            From the first, <m>C = -A = \dfrac{1}{16}</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
        <p>
          With <m>A = -\dfrac{1}{16}</m>, <m>B = \dfrac{1}{4}</m>, and <m>C = \dfrac{1}{16}</m>,
          the decomposition is:
          <me>\frac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \frac{-\frac{1}{16}}{s} + \frac{\frac{1}{4}}{s^2} + \frac{\frac{1}{16}}{s+4}.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <!-- ── TASK 2: COMPUTE ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-repeated-compute">
      <title>Step 2: Apply the Inverse Transform</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the decomposition from Step 1, compute
          <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1}{s^3+4s^2}\right\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Apply linearity to split into three separate inverse transforms, then match each to the table:
          <ul>
            <li><p>
              <m>\dfrac{-\frac{1}{16}}{s}</m>: use <xref ref="formula-A" text="custom">Formula A</xref>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{4}}{s^2}</m>: use <xref ref="formula-C" text="custom">Formula C</xref>
              with <m>n = 1</m>. Rewrite as <m>\dfrac{1}{4} \cdot \dfrac{1!}{s^{1+1}}</m>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              <m>\dfrac{\frac{1}{16}}{s+4}</m>: use <xref ref="formula-B" text="custom">Formula B</xref>
              with <m>a = -4</m>.
            </p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From the previous part, we found: 
          <me>\frac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \frac{-\frac{1}{16}}{s} + \frac{\frac{1}{4}}{s^2} + \frac{\frac{1}{16}}{s+4}.</me>
          Applying linearity and reading off
          <xref ref="formula-A" text="custom">Formula A</xref>,
          <xref ref="formula-C" text="custom">Formula C</xref> (with <m>n=1</m>),
          and <xref ref="formula-B" text="custom">Formula B</xref> (with <m>a=-4</m>):
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{s^2(s+4)}\right\}
              \amp= \underbrace{\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{-\frac{1}{16}}{s}\right\}}_{\text{Formula A}}
                   + \underbrace{\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{\frac{1}{4}}{s^2}\right\}}_{\text{Formula C},\, n=1}
                   + \underbrace{\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{\frac{1}{16}}{s+4}\right\}}_{\text{Formula B},\, a=-4}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= -\frac{1}{16} \cdot 1
                   + \frac{1}{4} \cdot \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1!}{s^{1+1}}\right\}
                   + \frac{1}{16}\,e^{-4t}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= -\frac{1}{16} + \frac{1}{4}t + \frac{1}{16}e^{-4t}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          A repeated linear factor <m>s^2</m> contributes two terms in the partial fraction form:
          one with <m>s</m> in the denominator and one with <m>s^2</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          After decomposing, match each term carefully: watch the value of <m>n</m> in
          <xref ref="formula-C" text="custom">Formula C</xref>
          and the sign of <m>a</m> in
          <xref ref="formula-B" text="custom">Formula B</xref>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

